          </zone-style>
        </zone>
        <zone forceUpdate='true' h='56778' id='12' type-v2='text' w='91833' x='2000' y='38667'>
          <formatted-text>
            <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book' fontsize='14'>Why These 3 States?</run>
            <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
            <run fontcolor='#14181f' fontname='Courier New' fontsize='12'>Florida, Texas, and New York represent the highest-priority states for EV infrastructure investment based on two key factors: high absolute EV registrations combined with below-average adoption rates compared to the national average (1.30%).</run>
            <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
            <run fontcolor='#14181f' fontname='Courier New' fontsize='12'>🔵 </run>
            <run bold='true' fontcolor='#14181f' fontname='Courier New' fontsize='12'>Florida</run>
            <run fontcolor='#14181f' fontname='Courier New' fontsize='12'> — </run>
            <run bold='true' fontcolor='#14181f' fontname='Courier New' fontsize='12'>254,900 EVs | 1.37% adoption</run>
            <run fontcolor='#14181f' fontname='Courier New' fontsize='12'>Æ </run>
            <run>Æ&#10;</run>
            <run fontcolor='#14181f' fontname='Courier New' fontsize='12'>Despite being the 3rd largest EV market by volume, Florida&apos;s adoption rate barely exceeds the national average. Its large population and sunny climate make it ideal for accelerated EV growth with the right infrastructure support.</run>
            <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
            <run fontcolor='#14181f' fontname='Courier New' fontsize='12'>🔵 </run>
            <run bold='true' fontcolor='#14181f' fontname='Courier New' fontsize='12'>Texas</run>
            <run fontcolor='#14181f' fontname='Courier New' fontsize='12'> — </run>
            <run bold='true' fontcolor='#14181f' fontname='Courier New' fontsize='12'>230,100 EVs | 0.89% adoption</run>
            <run>Æ&#10;</run>
            <run fontcolor='#14181f' fontname='Courier New' fontsize='12'>The largest infrastructure gap in the country. Texas has the 2nd highest EV count nationally but its adoption rate is 32% below the national average — a clear sign that charging infrastructure is not keeping pace with demand.</run>
            <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
            <run fontcolor='#14181f' fontname='Courier New' fontsize='12'>🔵 </run>
            <run bold='true' fontcolor='#14181f' fontname='Courier New' fontsize='12'>New York</run>
            <run fontcolor='#14181f' fontname='Courier New' fontsize='12'> — </run>
            <run bold='true' fontcolor='#14181f' fontname='Courier New' fontsize='12'>131,300 EVs | 1.16% adoption</run>
            <run>Æ&#10;</run>
            <run fontcolor='#14181f' fontname='Courier New' fontsize='12'>Dense urban corridors and high commuter traffic make New York a high-impact investment target. Closing the gap to California levels could add hundreds of thousands of EV users.</run>
          </formatted-text>
          <zone-style>
            <format attr='border-color' value='#4e79a7' />
            <format attr='border-style' value='solid' />
            <format attr='border-width' value='1' />
          </zone-style>
        </zone>
      </zones>
